Chapter 1 – Quick Start

	Chapter Contents
	1. Installing ccdb
	2. Loading ccdb modules
	3. Running ccdb

	This chapter describes how to start using ccdb in the minimal amount of
	time.

	1. Installing ccdb

	This tool is distributed as a PE package and should be installed by default.

	2. Loading ccdb modules

	From the command line, type:

		$ module load ccdb

	This loads the default module for ccdb that is installed on the system.

	3. Running ccdb

	After the ccdb module is loaded, allocate the compute node resources
	required for the debugging session. For example, if your site uses PBS,
	type:

		$ qsub –I –l mppwidth=W (where W is a scalar value)

	This command requests compute nodes from the system and starts an
	interactive session with access to the requested compute nodes.

	Next, issue the ccdb command to start the comparative debugger:

		$ ccdb
		ccdb - Cray Comparative Debugger
		Copyright (c) 2007-2011 Cray Inc. All Rights Reserved.
		Copyright (c) 1996-2011 by Guardsoft Pty Ltd
		dbg all>

	At this point, you are ready to begin entering ccdb commands. For example,
	to invoke an application to debug, type:

		dbg all> invoke $myapp[W] /path/to/app
								(Where W is the number of application nodes)

	As long as you have enough available idle compute nodes allocated, new
	invocations of an application can be issued using the invoke command. This
	will transparently launch the application onto the nodes using aprun. See
	chapter 4 of this manual for more information.


Chapter 2 - Introduction

	ccdb is a relative debugger
	Unlike other debuggers, ccdb allows application developers to compare data
	structures from many different applications as they are executing. This
	information can then be used as an aid in identifying the location of
	errors.

	ccdb is a conventional interactive parallel debugger
	ccdb can be used to debug parallel and serial applications using interactive
	commands, such as setting breakpoints and displaying the program state.
	Because ccdb is a parallel debugger, it allows debugging commands to be
	applied to sets of parallel processes.

	ccdb is built on top of gdb
	gdb is a versatile debugger that has widespread adoption, a large support
	community, and ongoing active development. gdb provides the low-level debug
	services with which ccdb interacts to enable the task of comparative
	debugging. The syntax used in ccdb has also been heavily influenced by the
	High Performance Debugging Forum’s HPD standard.


Chapter 3 – Definitions & Syntax

	The command set used in ccdb is derived from gdb. Users will find that the
	syntax and feel of the debugger is similar to that of gdb, the most notable
	difference being support for parallel applications which gdb currently
	lacks.

	For example, a typical command is formatted as:

		command [arg1]…[argN]

	Arguments to commands can consist of keywords or expressions.

	Expressions are comprised of the usual C-like operators, constants, internal
	variables, and program variables commonly used inside of gdb. Enclosing an
	expression with quotes denotes that this portion of the expression should be
	passed directly to the gdb backend and not interpreted by any built-in ccdb
	specific functions.

	ccdb supports convenience variables for storing any temporary variables to
	be used in a later expression. These variables are denoted by a dollar sign
	followed by a name; for example $var.

	When parallel applications are debugged using ccdb, the application ranks
	are arranged into groups called process sets. The default process set is
	named all and contains the ranks of every application that is currently
	being debugged. Users can refer to specific application ranks by defining a
	new process set containing the desired ranks and focusing on that named set.
	A user can also refer to every rank of a specific application by focusing on
	the named process set that was specified during the original invoke of the
	application. When a user focuses on a process set, it becomes the current
	set. Commands are only applied to ranks contained in the current set. To
	reiterate, by default the process set all is the current set and all
	commands issued inside ccdb will act upon every rank of the debugged
	applications.

	In ccdb, variables of a target application are referenced using a syntax
	consisting of an optional process set argument and an identifier or gdb
	expression, which is separated by ::. If no process set is declared, the
	command is sent to all ranks in the current set. For example, to refer to
	the variable var in the process set $s, use the argument $s::var. If var is
	a pointer that you want to dereference, enclose the expression in quotes;
	for example, $s::”*var”. The use of quotes denotes that the expression
	contains something that should be passed directly to gdb.

	If a convenience variable or target program variable is an array, specific
	elements can be referenced using standard C array syntax, such as
	$s::var[3][4]. ccdb also supports the notion of array slices. An array can
	be sliced by specifying an array index as a range, such as
	$s::var[3..10][4].


Chapter 4 - Usage

	Chapter Contents
	1. Application Invocation
	2. Comparisons and Assertions

	ccdb is started by issuing the ccdb command in a terminal. Once the client
	is started, you will see an introductory message, followed by a command
	prompt:

		$ ccdb
		ccdb - Cray Comparative Debugger
		Copyright (c) 2007-2011 Cray Inc. All Rights Reserved.
		Copyright (c) 1996-2011 by Guardsoft Pty Ltd
		dbg all>

	At this point, you can begin issuing commands to the debugger. To view a
	list of all valid commands, type help. Help text containing valid command
	syntax is displayed.


	1. Application Invocation

	New applications to debug are launched using the invoke command. Invoke
	specifies the name of the executable and the number of processes to create.

	The syntax of the invoke command is:

		invoke <process set expression> <application> [--args <arguments>]

	The process set expression argument is a list of one or more convenience
	variables that are used as the process set name(s) for the application.
	These convenience variables can either be a scalar, such as $app1, or arrays
	of a given length, such as $app2[8] or $app3[2][4]. If more than one
	convenience variable is provided, each should be separated using a comma.
	The summation of all convenience variables, including the length of any
	array, is used to determine the number of application ranks that will be
	launched.

	For example, if you provide the following process set expression:

		$app1,$app2[8],$app3[2][4]

	You will have 17 ranks in your application. This is calculated by summing:

		$app1 = 1 rank
		$app2[8] = 1*8 = 8 ranks
		$app3[2][4] = 1*2*4 = 8 ranks
		Total: 17 processes

	The total number of ranks is used transparently to create the aprun command
	and launch the application. The ranks of the application are assigned in
	ascending order to each declared convenience variable, in a left to right
	fashion. Currently there is no support for aprun specific arguments, but
	this is something that is planned to be added in the near future.


	2. Comparisons and Assertions

	Comparisons and assertions are commands that enable the task of comparative
	debugging. The compare command is used to instantaneously compare program
	data relative to the set of invoked applications. For example, consider two
	single rank applications that have been invoked with the convenience
	variable names $app1 and $app2. A breakpoint is set in both and the
	applications are instructed to continue until the breakpoint is reached. At
	this breakpoint, there is a variable named var in the current execution
	scope of the two applications that you want to compare. Do so by issuing the
	following command:

		compare $app1::var = $app2::var

	If the display mode is set to the default of “text” mode, the differences
	between these two variables are displayed immediately.

	The assertion command is employed when you want to define an equality
	condition to check for during application execution. In order to define
	assertions before application execution, use the graph command. Assertions
	are encapsulated inside of a graph, and defined using a variable reference
	similar to the syntax used in the compare command at a particular locale
	inside of the application source. The general syntax of an assertion looks
	like the following:

		graph $g
			assert $app1::var@”app1.f”:10 = $app2::var@”app2.f”:20
		end

	This command sequence tells the debugger to create a graph called $g 
	containing a single assertion command. This assertion compares the value of
	var from $app1 at line 10 in the source file “app1.f” to the value of var
	from $app2 at line 20 in the source file “app2.f”. Note that graphs can
	contain as many assertions as you want, but each additional assertion
	defined increases the time required to complete a full iteration of the
	graph. You can then issue the start $g command to instruct the debugger to 
	begin execution of the graph. Note that before an assertion can be defined
	or a graph run, applications that correspond to the assertion definitions in
	the graph must have been invoked previously and be at their initial startup
	barrier. When a graph is started, breakpoints are automatically inserted at
	the defined locale locations in the applications and both are continued.
	When a defined breakpoint is reached, the debugger extracts the contents of
	the defined variables in an architecture-neutral format and performs the
	comparison.


