	The following list describes the types of commands available in ccdb.

	Commands are classified into three target scope:

		* Application independent: The command does not interact with an
		  application.
		* Application targeted: The command interacts with an application.
		* Application optional: The command may or may not interact with an
		  application, depending on its arguments.

	These commands are used in coordination with two debugger modes:
	
		* Interactive mode: Commands encountered are executed as soon as they
		  are issued, generally resulting in client side actions being taken or
		  communication with debug servers taking place.
		* Deferred mode: Commands encountered are not executed, but rather saved
		  into an internal buffer and executed at a later time after leaving the
		  deferred mode context. In order to leave the deferred mode context,
		  the user should issue the end command. The only way to enter this mode
		  is to make use of a deferred mode command.

	Commands such as set and info are interactive mode commands, whereas
	commands such as graph and blockmap will switch the debugger from
	interactive mode to deferred mode. The end command switches the debugger
	from deferred mode back to interactive mode and the block of commands
	entered is ready to be executed.

	The commands defined in this chapter will take on the following form:

	Command name												(target scope)
	
		Summary: Brief summary of the command.
		Usage: command <arguments> [<optional argument>] [-<options>]
		Example command: command example

		Sub-commands: If valid.
		Arguments: Explanation of command arguments.

		Description:
		Detailed description of the command.

	Each command begins with its bolded name and target scope (one of the three
	defined target scopes). This is followed by a brief summary of the command,
	its usage including any possible arguments it expects or options, followed
	by a typical usage example of the command. Any sub-commands that are used in
	conjunction with this command are displayed, which is typical of deferred
	mode commands, as well as an explanation of the arguments defined in the
	commands usage. Finally a detailed description of the command is given.


	1. Interactive Mode Commands

	assign												(application optional)

		Summary: Assignment command.
		Usage: assign <target> <expr> [-force]
		Example command: assign j 20

		Arguments: target (target variable), expr (expression syntax), -force
		(force assignment)

		Description:
		Assign the value of an expression defined by expr to target. The target
		must evaluate to an l-value that is either a debugger variable (such as
		$a) or an application variable. If the target is the name of a debugger
		variable that does not exist, a new debugger variable with that name is
		automatically created. If the optional force option is specified, then
		the variable assignment is forced. This is used when the type of l-value
		defined by expr does not match the type of the l-value of target.

	backtrace											(application targeted)

		Summary: Print stack frame backtrace command.
		Usage: backtrace [<num>]
		Example command: backtrace

		Arguments: num (pos/neg num frames)

		Description:
		Display a backtrace of the stack frames for all application ranks in the
		current process set. If num is supplied, the innermost num frames are
		displayed. If num is negative, the outermost num frames are displayed.

	break												(application targeted)

		Summary: Set breakpoint command.
		Usage: break <location>
		Example command: break main.c:20

		Arguments: location (line number, function name, or address)

		Description:
		Set a breakpoint in every process of the current process set. The
		location of the breakpoint can be defined as a line number, a function
		name, or “*” and an address. If an address is specified, it must begin
		with an asterix (*). For example, break *0x0000000000400693 would break
		at address 0x400693.

	blockmap										(application independent)

		Summary: Blockmap creation command.
		Usage: blockmap <name>(<process>::<variable>)
		Example command: blockmap decomposition(P::V)

		Deferred mode commands: define, end, help
		Arguments: name (named variable), process (process set reference),
		variable (variable reference)

		Description:
		Begin creation of a new data decomposition scheme. Blockmap sub-commands
		are read until end is encountered. Blockmap is used to specify the
		relationship of distributed variables inside of an assertion between
		instances of applications. When the blockmap function is used in an
		assertion, it is supplied with a process set argument and a variable
		argument to operate on. Typically a blockmap is used inside of a
		dataflow graph definition in order to compare variables from two
		separate process sets that are the same size but of a different
		distribution shape.

		To create a blockmap, the user must provide a data definition describing
		the size and dimension of the distributed variable. The user must also
		provide a distribute definition for each dimension of the variable
		containing either block, cyclic, or * (star). This describes how the
		data in each of the variables dimensions are distributed amongst the
		application ranks.

		Block is used when a contiguous number of elements of the specified
		dimension (row or column) will be mapped to a different processor (i.e.
		high order bits of the index dictate the processor number). Cyclic is
		used when each element of the given dimension is mapped to a processor
		in a circular manner (i.e. low order bits of the index dictate the
		processor number). * (star) is used when the decomposition for that
		dimension is ignored (i.e. each rank receives the entire set of indexes
		in the specified dimension).

	compare												(application optional)

		Summary: Compare command.
		Usage: compare <expr1> = <expr2>
		Example command: compare i = j

		Arguments: expr1 (variable expression), expr2 (variable expression)

		Description:
		Compare (subtract) the value of expression expr1 with the value of
		expression expr2. The values must be of the same size and shape, or a
		translation function (such as a blockmap) must be used. For complex data
		types such as arrays and structures, the comparison is performed on the
		zeroeth element only. The output of this command depends on the settings
		of display, output, and error variables.

	continue											(application targeted)

		Summary: Continue debugging application.
		Usage: continue
		Example command: continue

		Arguments: None.

		Description:
		Continue execution of every process in the current process set from its
		current location.

	create											(application independent)

		Summary: Debugger variable creation command.
		Usage: create <varname>[[<dim>]]
		Example command: create $new_var

		Arguments: varname (new named debugger variable), dim (optional array
		dimensions)

		Description:
		Create an internal debugger variable with the name varname. If the
		dimension is specified, the debugger variable will be an array of the
		specified size and shape.

	defset												(application targeted)

		Summary: Define process set command.
		Usage: defset <name> <process set expression>
		Example command: defset new_set $a,$b[3]

		Arguments: name (new process set name), process set expression (process
		set expression list)

		Description:
		Create a new process set of application ranks that can be focused upon.
		The values of the process set expression must be valid invoked
		application names and valid ranks if a particular rank is targeted. A
		set cannot contain other process sets defined through defset.

	delete												(application targeted)

		Summary: Delete breakpoint command.
		Usage: delete [<breakpoints>]
		Example command: delete 1

		Arguments: breakpoints (list of breakpoint numbers)

		Description:
		Delete the list of breakpoints specified by the breakpoints argument in
		every application rank of the current process set. If no argument is
		supplied, all breakpoints in the current process set are deleted.

	dfinfo											(application independent)

		Summary: Dfinfo command.
		Usage: dfinfo [<subcommand>]
		Example command: dfinfo

		Arguments: subcommand (dfinfo subcommand)

		Description:
		Display information related to comparative debugging dataflow commands.
		If no argument is given, display a list of all valid dfinfo subcommands.

	down												(application targeted)

		Summary: Move stack frame down command.
		Usage: down [<num>]
		Example command: down

		Arguments: num (number of frames to go down)

		Description:
		Move the call stack down by num frames. If no argument is supplied, then
		move down by one frame.

	focus											(application independent)

		Summary: Focus process set command.
		Usage: focus <proc_set>
		Example command: focus all

		Arguments: proc_set (named process set)

		Description:
		Change the focus of the current process set to the named process set
		specified by the argument proc_set.

	graph											(application independent)

		Summary: Dataflow graph creation command.
		Usage: graph <name>
		Example command: graph $my_graph

		Deferred mode commands: assert, assign, set, help, end
		Arguments: name (debugger convenience variable)

		Description:
		Begin creation of a new dataflow graph. Graph sub-commands are read
		until end is encountered.

	halt												(application targeted)

		Summary: Halt all executing processes.
		Usage: halt
		Example command: halt

		Arguments: None.

		Description:
		Halt all executing processes in the current process set. This is
		equivalent to sending a SIGINT to the processes.

	help											(application independent)

		Summary: Help command.
		Usage: help [<command>]
		Example command: help

		Arguments: command (command specific message)

		Description:
		Display help information about commands. If no argument is given,
		display a list of all valid commands.

	info											(application independent)

		Summary: Info command.
		Usage: info [<subcommand>]
		Example command: info

		Arguments: subcommand (info subcommand)

		Description:
		Display information relating to the application being debugged. If no
		argument is given, display a list of all valid info subcommands.

	invoke											(application independent)

		Summary: Specify an application to be debugged.
		Usage: invoke <proc_list> <application> [--args <args>]
		Example command: invoke $a ./helloworld

		Arguments: proc_list (debugger convenience variable syntax), application
		(name of application), args (application arguments)

		Description:
		Start an application with process set name of proc_list and optional
		application arguments args. For sequential programs, proc_list is a
		single scalar convenience variable. For parallel programs, proc_list
		must be a list of one or more convenience variable arrays or multiple
		scalar convenience variables. Application ranks are assigned to each
		convenience variable in the proc_list sequentially, starting from the
		left.

		The summation of all convenience variables, including the length of any
		array, is used to determine the number of application ranks that will be
		launched.

		For example:
		A serial application with a proc_list of $a,$b,$c would start 3 distinct
		application instances. A parallel application with a proc_list of $a[9]
		would start a single distinct application instance containing 9
		application ranks. A parallel application with a proc_list of $a,$b[8]
		would start a single distinct application instance with 9 ranks that are
		separated into two process sets. Process set $a would contain one rank
		and process set $b would contain eight ranks.

	kill												(application targeted)

		Summary: Kill invoked applications.
		Usage: kill
		Example command: kill

		Arguments: None.

		Description:
		Kill all processes contained in the current process set.

	list												(application targeted)

		Summary: List specified function or line command.
		Usage: list [-] [<expr>]
		Example command: list 20

		Arguments: - (backward direction), expr (list expression syntax)

		List expression syntax

		The list expression can contain an argument of the following forms:

			1. linenum - display source lines around specified line number.
			2. file:linenum - display source lines around specified line number
			   in specified source file.
			3. function - display source lines around specified function.
			4. file:function - display source lines around specified function in
			   specified source file.
			5. *addr - display source lines around the line corresponding to
			   address addr.

		Additionally, a list of two arguments can be defined in expr (separated
		with ,) to display all source lines between arg1 and arg2.

		Description:
		Display source code of the processes in the current process set. With no
		argument, ten lines of code are displayed from the previous listing.
		If - is specified, ten previous lines of code are displayed.

	next												(application targeted)

		Summary: Next command.
		Usage: next [<num>]
		Example command: next

		Arguments: num (number of lines to advance)

		Description:
		Step program, but do not follow into subroutine calls. If the argument
		num is supplied, advance num lines.

	print												(application optional)

		Summary: Print the value of an expression.
		Usage: print <expr>
		Example command: print prog_variable

		Arguments: expr (expression to print)

		Description:
		Print the value of expr for each application rank in the current process
		set. The expr can contain any combination of debugger convenience or
		application variables. Valid application variables are those of the
		lexical environment of each ranks selected stack frame, plus all those
		whose scope is global or over an entire file.

	quit											(application independent)

		Summary: Quit command.
		Usage: quit
		Example command: quit

		Arguments: None.

		Description:
		Quit the debugger.

	release												(application targeted)

		Summary: Release debugged applications from debugger control.
		Usage: release <process_set>
		Example command: release $a

		Arguments: process_set (named process set)

		Description:
		Release debugged applications specified by process_set from debugger
		control.

	restart												(application targeted)

		Summary: Dataflow graph restart command.
		Usage: restart <name>
		Example command: restart $my_graph

		Arguments: name (defined graph name)

		Description:
		Restart execution of a defined dataflow graph. All active applications
		are killed and restarted before graph execution begins.

	set												(application independent)

		Summary: Set command.
		Usage: set [<subcommand>]
		Example command: set

		Arguments: subcommand (set subcommand)

		Description:
		Set information relating to ccdb environment settings and behavior. If
		no argument is given, display a list of all valid set subcommands.

	show											(application independent)

		Summary: Show command.
		Usage: show [<subcommand>]
		Example command: show

		Arguments: subcommand (show subcommand)

		Description:
		Show information relating to ccdb environment settings and behavior. If
		no argument is given, display a list of all valid show subcommands.

	sleep											(application independent)

		Summary: Sleep command.
		Usage: sleep [<secs>]
		Example command: sleep 5

		Arguments: secs (seconds to sleep)

		Description:
		Pause the debugger for secs seconds. If secs is omitted, the debugger
		will sleep for a default of five seconds.

	source											(application independent)

		Summary: Read and execute debugger commands.
		Usage: source <filename>
		Example command: source myfile

		Arguments: filename (path to named command file)

		Description:
		Read debugger commands from a file to automate debugging tasks.

	start											(application independent)

		Summary: Dataflow graph execution command.
		Usage: start <name>
		Example command: start $my_graph

		Arguments: name (defined graph name)

		Description:
		Begin execution of a defined dataflow graph. Graph execution cycles
		until the dataflow interpreter halts due to encountering assertion
		failures or program errors.

	step												(application targeted)

		Summary: Step command.
		Usage: step [<num>]
		Example command: step

		Arguments: num (number of lines to step)

		Description:
		Advance the program to the next source line, following into subroutine
		calls. If the argument num is supplied, then advance num lines.

	stop											(application independent)

		Summary: Dataflow graph halt command.
		Usage: stop
		Example command: stop

		Arguments: None.

		Description:
		Inform the dataflow interpreter to halt the current graph execution
		immediately. This command is used to abort graph execution.

	up													(application targeted)

		Summary: Move stack frame up command.
		Usage: up [<num>]
		Example command: up

		Arguments: num (number of frames to go up)

		Description:
		Move the call stack up by num frames. If no argument is supplied, then
		move up by one frame.

	viewset											(application independent)

		Summary: View process set command.
		Usage: viewset [<proc_set>]
		Example command: viewset

		Arguments: proc_set (named process set)

		Description:
		Display information about defined process sets. With no argument
		provided, information about all defined process sets is displayed.

	whatis												(application targeted)

		Summary: Whatis data type of expression.
		Usage: whatis <expr>
		Example command: whatis prog_var

		Arguments: expr (expression syntax)

		Expression syntax

		The expr argument can be the name of a program variable in an invoked
		application or an internal debugger convenience variable such as $a.

		Description:
		Display the data type of an expression for each application rank in the
		current process set.


	2. Dfinfo Subcommands

	dfinfo assert

		Summary: Dataflow graph assert command.

		Description:
		Display help information regarding the use of dataflow graph assert
		commands. The assert command is only valid while defining a graph in
		deferred mode.

	dfinfo assign

		Summary: Dataflow graph assign command.

		Description:
		Display help information regarding the use of dataflow graph assign
		commands. The assign command is only valid while defining a graph in
		deferred mode.

	dfinfo diff

		Summary: Saved graph diff information.

		Description:
		Display the set of saved difference data from graph assertions collected
		during graph execution. A numeric argument can be provided to view the
		actual deltas of the data type comparison.

	dfinfo graph

		Summary: Display defined dataflow graphs.

		Description:
		Display the names of defined dataflow graphs. A name argument can be
		provided to view the assertions defined in the named graph.

	dfinfo status

		Summary: Graph status information.

		Description:
		Display information relating to actively executing graphs.


	3. Info Subcommands

	info breakpoints

		Summary: Breakpoint information.

		Description:
		Display the status of user defined breakpoints.

		The type column indicates one of the following:

			breakpoint - normal breakpoint
			watchpoint - watchpoint

		The disp column contains keep, del, or dis to indicate the disposition
		of the breakpoint after it is hit. If disp is displayed as dis, this
		means that the breakpoint will be disabled. The address and what columns
		indicate the address and file/line number of the breakpoint
		respectively.

	info blockmap

		Summary: Display defined blockmaps.

		Description:
		Display the names of defined blockmaps.

	info define

		Summary: Blockmap define command.

		Description:
		Display help information regarding the use of blockmap define commands.
		The define command is only valid while defining a blockmap in deferred
		mode.

	info locals

		Summary: Local symbol information.

		Description:
		Display a list of the local symbols in the current stack frame for each
		application rank in the current process set.

	info process

		Summary: Process information.

		Description:
		Display information related to currently invoked processes. The name
		column specifies the process set name used to identify the invoked
		application. The type column indicates if the process is serial or
		parallel. The procs column indicates the number of application ranks.
		The focus column indicates if this applications process set is currently
		focused upon. The command column identifies the command that was passed
		to aprun in order to launch the application.

	info status

		Summary: Status command.

		Description:
		Display the status of invoked applications.


	4. Set Subcommands

	set async

		Summary: Async mode.
		Usage: set async <on|off>
		Example command: set async on

		Default: on
		Arguments: on or off

		Description:
		Turn asynchronous mode on or off. This determines if the debugger will
		accept additional commands before the current command completes.

	set dfmode

		Summary: Dataflow processing mode.
		Usage: set dfmode <default|sync|async>
		Example command: set dfmode default

		Scope: Graph
		Default setting: default
		Arguments: default or sync or async

		Description:
		Set the dataflow processing mode to synchronous or asynchronous. This
		changes the dataflow graph that was generated using the graph command so
		that it operates in one of these three modes. Synchronous mode is
		default for assertions that refer to independent processes. Asynchronous
		mode is default for assertions that refer to the same process. Using
		this setting overrides the default behavior.

	set directory

		Summary: Directory search source path.
		Usage: set directory <path>
		Example command: set directory /path/to/source

		Arguments: path (directory containing source)

		Description:
		Add the directory defined by path to the beginning of the search path
		for the application source files that were used to generate symbol
		information for application. Typically this is used when an application
		was compiled on a different machine than run, but the source files used
		to compile the application are available at a new location. This will
		cause cached information about source file locations and line positions
		to be lost.

	set display

		Summary: Dataflow graph assert display mode.
		Usage: set display <text|aif|hdf|hdf5>
		Example command: set display text

		Scope: Graph
		Default setting: text
		Arguments: text or aif or hdf or hdf5

		Description:
		Set the display format for the result of dataflow graph assertions. In
		text mode, any differences are displayed as plaintext. In aif mode,
		differences are displayed in architecture independent format (AIF). In
		hdf and hdf5 modes, differences are displayed in the hierarchical data
		format (HDF) version 4 and version 5 respectively.

	set error

		Summary: Dataflow graph assert lower and upper floating point error 
		limits.
		Usage: set error <lower limit> <upper limit> [relative|absolute]
		Example command: set error 0.5 0.5 absolute

		Scope: Graph
		Default setting: ulp ulp absolute
		Where ulp is the smallest non-zero floating point value of the current 
		machine.
		Arguments: lower limit (float), upper limit (float), relative or 
		absolute

		Description:
		Set the tolerance level for the dataflow graph assertion difference
		deltas when comparing floating point values. Differences with deltas
		below the lower limit are ignored. Differences with deltas between the
		lower limit and the upper limit will be displayed using the current
		display mode setting. Any differences above the upper limit will halt
		the dataflow interpreter and stop graph execution. The relative and
		absolute limits are used to specify how the lower and upper limits
		should be interpreted. If relative is selected, the difference deltas
		between values in an assertion is divided by the larger of the original
		two values used to calculate the delta.

	set force

		Summary: Dataflow graph assert force mode.
		Usage: set force [on|off]
		Example command: set force off

		Scope: Graph
		Default setting: off
		Arguments: on or off

		Description:
		Determines whether subsequent dataflow graph assertions will copy the
		value of the left-variable (LHS) into the right-variable (RHS) after the
		assertion has finished executing.

	set function

		Summary: Set function definition command.
		Usage: set function <function_name>(<function_args>) = <expr>
		Example command: set function myfunc(myvar) = 1

		Arguments: function_name (name of function), function_args (function 
		arguments), expr (function expression definition)

		Description:
		Define a function macro named function_name. The expression expr can
		contain format arguments that are substituted by actual arguments
		supplied to the function. Recursive function expression calls are not
		allowed.

	set halt

		Summary: Dataflow interpreter halting mode.
		Usage: set halt [true|false]
		Example command: set halt true

		Scope: Graph
		Default setting: true
		Arguments: true or false

		Description:
		Determines if application ranks are halted when the dataflow interpreter
		terminates.

	set logfile

		Summary: Dataflow event logfile name.
		Usage: set logfile <filename>
		Example command: set logfile "my.file"

		Scope: Graph
		Default setting: null
		Arguments: filename (quoted string)

		Description:
		Specifies the name of the file to send dataflow event logging entries if
		dataflow event logging is turned on. Note that the provided filename
		must be enclosed by quotes.

	set logging

		Summary: Dataflow event logging mode.
		Usage: set logging [on|off]
		Example command: set logging on

		Scope: Graph
		Default setting: off
		Arguments: on or off

		Description:
		Turn dataflow event logging on or off.

	set output

		Summary: dataflow graph assertion result logfile name.
		Usage: set output <filename>
		Example command: set output "my.file"

		Scope: Graph
		Default setting: null
		Arguments: filename (quoted string)

		Description:
		Specify a file to send the results from dataflow graph assertions to.
		Note that only difference delta information from the execution of an
		assertion will be written. The provided filename must be enclosed by
		quotes.

	set reduce

		Summary: Dataflow reduce mode.
		Usage: set reduce [sum|product|mean|aphash|fnvhash|bobhash|none] 
		[<lower precision>] [<upper precision>]
		Example command: set reduce aphash

		Scope: Graph
		Arguments: sum or product or mean or aphash or fnvhash or bobhash or 
		none, lower precision (float), upper precision (float)

		Description:
		Use a data reduction function to reduce the volume of data sent from
		compute nodes. There are both simple and complex functions currently
		supported for data reduction. The simple functions are sum, product, and
		mean which compute the sum, product, or mean of the data structure in
		question.

		Currently there are three hash functions supported which will produce
		more reliable results. Jenkins hash function (bobhash), Fowler–Noll–Vo
		hash function (fnvhash), and Arash Partow hash function (aphash). Note
		that these hashing functions are for data reduction purposes only and
		are not cryptographically secure. When hashing is used, the two original
		sets of data will no longer be directly compared. Instead, two sets of
		hashed signatures will be compared. If a blockmap is used to compare a
		serial data structure to an n-ary distributed data structure, then the
		serial program will calculate n hashes and compare it with its
		associated counterpart in the n-ary distributed data structure.

		If floating point values are being used with a hashing function, the
		user can specify the precision tolerance (lower and upper) for the hash
		function using the optional lower precision and upper precision
		arguments. For example, issuing the command set reduce bobhash 1 2 will
		set the reduce function to Jenkins hash with floating point lower
		precision and upper precision of 10^-1 and 10^-2 respectively.


