	6. Blockmap Deferred Mode Commands

	define

		Summary: Blockmap define command.
		Usage: define [data|distribute](<dimensional definition>)
		Example command: define distribute(block,*)

		Arguments: data or distribute, dimensional definition 
		(dimension/rank/distribution information)

		Description:
		Define a blockmap data or distribute definition. The data definition is
		used to define the number of dimensions of the data type and the range
		(or number of indexes) in each dimension. The distribute definition is
		used to define how the data is distributed amongst different ranks in
		each dimension of the data type in an application. The dimensions of the
		supplied data and distribute definitions must match. Thus if the user
		supplies data(32,64,1024) they must also supply a distribute function
		defining distribution behavior in each of these three dimensions.

		The data definition accepts a numerical list of input which defines how
		many indexes are found in the dimensions. Each dimension is separated
		with a comma in the list.

		The distribute definition accepts either block, cyclic, or * for each of
		its dimensions. A block is used when contiguous elements of the
		specified dimension (row or column) will be mapped to a different
		application rank (i.e. high order bits of the index dictate the
		application rank number). A cyclic is used when each index of the given
		dimension is mapped to each application rank in a circular manner (i.e.
		low order bits of the index dictate the application rank number). A *
		(star) is used when the decomposition for that dimension is ignored
		(i.e. each application rank receives the entire set of indexes in the
		specified dimension).

		The user is required to define both a data definition and distribute
		definition to successfully declare a blockmap.

	end

		Summary: Blockmap end command.
		Usage: end
		Example command: end

		Arguments: None.

		Description:
		This command is used to end the current blockmap definition. After
		issuing end, the debugger will exit deferred mode and attempt to create
		a blockmap function for future use. The user will be warned if there
		were any errors that prevented the blockmap function from being created.

	help

		Summary: Help command.

		Description:
		Display help information about blockmap deferred mode commands.

	7. Graph Deferred Mode Commands

	assert

		Summary: Dataflow graph assert command.
		Usage: assert <expr1> = <expr2>
		Example command:
		assert $app1::var1@"app1.c":10 = $app2::var2@"app2.c":20

		Arguments: expr1 (assert expr syntax), expr2 (assert expr syntax)

		Assert expression syntax

		Expr Usage: <procset>::<var>@”<source file>”:<line>
		Expr arguments: procset (defined process set name), var (application
		variable), source file (quoted source file name), line (line in source
		file to reference var)
		Expr example: $my_app::my_var@"my_source.c":10

		Description:
		Assert that the variable var defined in expr1 is equivalent to the
		variable var defined in expr2. Variables are specified using the assert
		expression syntax which declares both spatial and temporal references
		for a variable.

		Define the spatial locality with $procset::var to specify the particular
		variable of interest within a set of application ranks. This is followed
		with the temporal reference of "source file":line to define the point
		within application execution where to examine the value of the variable.

		The result of the assertion command depends on the current settings of
		error, display, output, and logging.

	assign

		Summary: Dataflow variable assignment command.
		Usage: assign <convenience var> <application var> [-force]
		Example command: assign $saved_var $procset::var

		Arguments: convenience var (debugger convenience variable), application
		var (application variable), -force (optional force mode)

		Description:
		Assign the value of the application variable application var to internal
		debugger convenience variable convenience var. The saved application
		variable will then be available for use in later expressions during
		application execution.

	end

		Summary: Dataflow graph end command.
		Usage: end
		Example command: end

		Arguments: None.

		Description:
		This command is used to end the current graph definition. After issuing
		end, the debugger will exit deferred mode and attempt to compile the
		defined graph with the dataflow compiler. The user will be warned if
		there were any errors that prevented the graph from being compiled. Upon
		successful compilation, the graph can be run by the dataflow interpreter
		by issuing the start command.

	help

		Summary: Help command.

		Description:
		Display help information about graph deferred mode commands.

	set

		Summary: Set command.
		Usage: set [<subcommand>]
		Example command: set

		Arguments: subcommand (set subcommand)

		Description:
		Set information relevant to the scope of the dataflow graph relating to
		ccdb environment settings and behavior. If no argument is given, display
		a list of all valid set subcommands.






